---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const title = 'Install';
const description =
  'Install the Groundwork skill in any AI coding harness. 50+ supported. Plus the optional detector CLI for CI.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="container install">
    <h1>Install Groundwork</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      One command installs the skill. The installer detects your harness and
      drops the files where they belong.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Install the skill</h2>
    <p>
      Works anywhere the open
      <a href="https://github.com/vercel-labs/skills">skills</a> installer
      runs: Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, and
      <a href="https://github.com/vercel-labs/skills#supported-agents">50+ more</a>.
    </p>

    <pre><code>npx skills add IgniticAB/groundwork</code></pre>

    <p>Open the agent, type <code>groundwork</code>, and the menu appears.</p>

    <h3>Or, in Claude Code, install it as a plugin</h3>
    <pre><code>/plugin install groundwork --marketplace IgniticAB/groundwork</code></pre>
    <p>
      Same skill, distributed through Claude Code's own plugin system. Either
      path works; the plugin is the one that keeps itself updated.
    </p>

    <h3>Target a specific harness</h3>
    <pre><code>npx skills add IgniticAB/groundwork -a claude-code
npx skills add IgniticAB/groundwork -a cursor -a codex</code></pre>

    <h3>Install globally</h3>
    <pre><code>npx skills add IgniticAB/groundwork -g</code></pre>

  <h2>If your format check fails right after installing</h2>
  <p>
    The installer writes a <code>skills-lock.json</code> at your repo root with
    two-space indentation, and rewrites it on every <code>skills update</code>.
    A formatter that covers JSON with different settings — Prettier with
    <code>useTabs: true</code>, for instance — will fail on it. Treat it like
    any other generated lockfile and add it to the ignore list:
  </p>
  <pre><code># .prettierignore
skills-lock.json</code></pre>

    <h2>2. Use it</h2>
    <p>Three ways to invoke a command:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>By name.</strong> <code>groundwork init</code>, <code>groundwork audit</code>, <code>groundwork plan</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>By natural language.</strong> <em>"Set up this repo for AI-first development"</em> triggers <code>init</code>. The skill's description routes to the right command.</li>
      <li><strong>By slash shortcut</strong> (Claude Code only). <code>/gw init</code>, <code>/gw audit</code>. Ships with the skill.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>On a fresh repo:</p>
    <pre><code>groundwork init</code></pre>
    <p>On a repo with stale context files:</p>
    <pre><code>groundwork document</code></pre>

    <h2>3. Add the CLI for CI (optional)</h2>
    <p>
      The skill authors context. The CLI guards it. Twenty deterministic
      rules, no LLM, no network, no key — so it runs in a pre-commit hook and
      in CI with a stable exit code. Wire it in and context drift never ships.
    </p>
    <pre><code>npx @ignitic/groundwork detect --fail-on P0</code></pre>
    <p>
      Every flag and the twenty rules: <a href={url('cli/')}>the CLI page</a>.
      Pull skill updates with <code>npx @ignitic/groundwork skills update</code>.
    </p>

    <Callout label="A note on memory" tone="mute">
      <p>
        Groundwork governs <strong>context</strong>: the files your repo
        commits. It does not govern <strong>memory</strong>: your agent's
        long-term notes about you. Personal preferences belong in memory, not
        in <code>CLAUDE.md</code>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <style>
    .install pre { max-width: 100%; }
    .install h2 { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-top: 2rem; }
    .install h2:first-of-type { border-top: none; padding-top: 0; }
  </style>
</Base>
